  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Determine all Pythagorean triples for which the hypotenuse is one unit longer than one of its sides.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(2k+1,2k^2+2k,2k^2+2k+1)$ for positive integers $k$, allowing the two legs to be interchanged.\n\nWrite the triple as $(m,n,n+1)$; then $n=\\frac{(m^2-1)}{2}$, so $m$ must be odd.",
      "worked": "Write such a triple as $(m,n,n+1)$, allowing the two legs to be interchanged.\n$$\\begin{align*}m^2+n^2&=(n+1)^2\\\\m^2+n^2&=n^2+2n+1\\\\m^2&=2n+1\\\\n&=\\frac{m^2-1}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $n$ to be a positive integer, $m$ must be an odd integer at least 3. Write $m=2k+1$, where $k\\geq1$ is an integer.\n$$\\begin{align*}n&=\\frac{(2k+1)^2-1}{2}\\\\n&=\\frac{4k^2+4k}{2}\\\\n&=2k^2+2k.\\end{align*}$$\nThus all the triples have the form $(2k+1,2k^2+2k,2k^2+2k+1)$. Conversely, these are positive integers and $m^2=2n+1$ gives $m^2+n^2=(n+1)^2$, so every positive integer $k$ gives a valid triple.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 110
  },
